Implementation prompt for the AI website builder
Document type: Page-level frontend, backend, scientific-data and interaction contract
Target location: My Farms → [Selected Farm] → Advanced in the farm's left-hand navigation
Scope: Implement the Advanced workspace for the selected farm with exactly three areas: Deep Analysis, Reports & Analysis History, and Automation & Monitoring. Do not create separate Field Analysis or Trends & Comparisons pages.
Design source: Field Shift Master Product, UX, Intelligence and Engineering Plan v8, especially the Farm Detail workspace, Farm Intelligence / Deep Analysis, Automation, scientific variable catalogue, data-source registry, canonical analysis pipeline and report-history requirements.
Important: This is a proposed implementation plan. It does not claim that every listed API is already connected or that every scientific metric can be calculated for every farm.

0. BUILDER INSTRUCTIONS — READ BEFORE CODING
You are implementing a farm-specific Advanced workspace, not creating a new global dashboard and not redesigning the rest of Field Shift.

The user opens My Farms, selects a farm, and is taken to that farm's workspace. Its existing local sidebar contains options such as Overview, Fields & Map, Crop Rotation Plan, Soil & Land, Water Intelligence, Weather & Climate, History & Reports, Advanced and Farm Settings. Selecting Advanced must open the feature described in this document.

The Advanced workspace must use the selected farm's real records and selected field context. It must never show data from a different farm, a different user, a demo farm or a previous selection.

0.1 Core purpose
Advanced contains exactly three areas:

How is this farm doing, what needs attention, and why? — Deep Analysis.
What did previous assessments find, and what changed between reports? — Reports & Analysis History.
Can Field Shift reassess the farm automatically and notify the owner about important changes? — Automation & Monitoring.
Do not create standalone Field Analysis or Trends & Comparisons areas. Deep Analysis must still include results for each field selected for a farm-wide run; those results belong inside the Deep Analysis report, not on a separate page. Historical observations and comparison with the previous report may support a Deep Analysis finding, but they must not become a separate trends dashboard, tab, or API.

The experience must feel like a professional agricultural intelligence workspace. It should translate complex environmental and farming data into a clear assessment, then allow the user to inspect the underlying numbers, evidence, formulas and limitations.

0.2 Non-negotiable scientific and product rules
The deterministic scientific calculation engine is the source of truth for numeric measurements, indices, risk rules, scores and recommendations.
AI may parse farmer-written information and explain validated results. AI must not invent measurements, calculate an unsupported score, diagnose a disease from a single signal, change a deterministic result or create an unverified alert.
Missing information is Unknown or Unavailable, not zero and not an assumed ideal value.
A source that is planned, unconfigured, unavailable or unverified must never be shown as connected or live.
Distinguish measured, farmer-reported, remotely sensed, modelled, reanalysis, forecast, estimated and calculated information.
Preserve source observation time separately from API retrieval time.
Every important number must have a unit, time period, source and evidence record. Add depth and spatial resolution where relevant.
A coarse grid value must not be described as a precise measurement of a small field.
A saved irrigation routine is not proof that irrigation actually happened. Store routines separately from confirmed irrigation events.
Observed rainfall, forecast rainfall and modelled effective rainfall are different inputs. Never merge them silently.
Each completed report is immutable. A new assessment creates a new report; it never rewrites a previous report.
Farm-level summaries must not hide a serious problem in one individual field.
A health status, a crop suitability score, data coverage and evidence confidence are four separate concepts. Never combine them into one unexplained number.
If a metric or model is not scientifically defensible with the available inputs, show an honest unavailable/insufficient-data state and explain what is missing.
0.3 Existing product architecture and deployment reality
Before coding, inspect the actual current repository and identify its framework, routes, existing farm/field schema, local persistence, existing API services and component library. Reuse valid existing services and data models. Do not create a second farm store or a second analysis engine.

The v8 code audit described a Vite/React client, an Express server and browser-local persistence, with only NASA POWER monthly climate and Open-Meteo current-weather requests visibly implemented in the inspected source snapshot. It also identified several provider names listed in the interface without matching retrieval/processing connectors, a simulated API test, and timer-driven fake analysis progress. Treat these as audit findings to verify against the current repository; do not assume they have already been fixed.

Do not claim that a listed API is integrated until a real request, response validation, data transformation and test have succeeded. Create a provider registry with the states planned, configured, available, degraded, unavailable and disabled.

For manual analysis, use the existing local-first persistence approach where that remains the approved deployment mode. For recurring automatic analysis while the browser is closed, a durable backend scheduler and worker are required. A browser timer, setInterval, service-worker timer or open-tab task is not a substitute for a durable scheduled job.

Do not silently add a hosted database, authentication product, cloud storage, remote farm-data processing or background job service to a local-only release. If the project owner explicitly enables connected scheduling, implement the server-side architecture described in Section 12, with explicit user consent and a visible data-transfer explanation. Otherwise build the full UI and service contracts, but show Automation as unavailable with a precise reason rather than pretending it works.

