3. DEEP ANALYSIS — COMPLETE FARM ASSESSMENT
This is the main feature inside Advanced.

3.1 Purpose
Deep Analysis answers:

How are the selected farm and its fields doing, what has changed, what potential risks exist, what evidence supports those findings, and what should the farmer consider doing next?

It combines the farm/field profiles, confirmed farming routines and events, current and historical weather, soil information, water calculations, valid satellite observations and documented agricultural rules.

The language model is not the source of truth for the assessment. The deterministic calculation engine must produce the metrics, status rules, findings and evidence references first. AI may then explain those validated results in simpler language.

3.2 Analysis scope selector
Support the following scopes:

type AnalysisScope =
  | { type: "whole_farm"; farmId: string }
  | { type: "all_fields"; farmId: string; fieldIds: string[] }
  | { type: "selected_fields"; farmId: string; fieldIds: string[] }
  | { type: "single_field"; farmId: string; fieldId: string };
Rules:

Whole farm includes farm-wide context and all eligible fields, but it must still calculate and display field-level results.
A field-only run must be labelled Field assessment, not Full farm assessment.
If a farm has multiple fields with different soil profiles, crops or irrigation systems, calculate them independently.
Never average raw values from fields that have incompatible units, crops, depths, time windows or source semantics.
The farm summary must show assessed field count and must not hide an Alert-level field behind a positive farm average.
The user must be able to include/exclude fields before starting.
3.3 Pre-analysis confirmation flow
Before starting a run, display a preflight drawer or page with:

Selected farm and fields.
Analysis date/window.
Data sources that will be queried and their current integration state.
Existing farmer routines that will be used.
Recent confirmed management events.
Missing information that could materially reduce coverage.
Whether data will be sent to an external provider or remote backend.
Estimated run type: local/manual, remote/manual or scheduled job.
Ask a short update question when relevant:

Has your watering routine changed?
Have you recorded any recent irrigation?
Have you planted, harvested or changed the crop?
Have you applied fertiliser or made another relevant management change?
Have you noticed a new issue in this field?
Do not force users to repeat the entire farm setup questionnaire. Prefill known values with their source and last-confirmed date. Make the user review changes before they affect analysis.

Natural-language practice entry
Allow the farmer to type something like:

I usually water every three days for around two hours.
AI may parse this into a draft object:

interface ParsedPracticeDraft {
  activity: "irrigation" | "planting" | "fertiliser" | "tillage" | "other";
  recurrence?: string;
  durationMinutes?: number;
  amount?: number;
  amountUnit?: string;
  method?: string;
  fieldIds: string[];
  effectiveFrom?: string;
  originalText: string;
  parsedFields: Record<string, unknown>;
  ambiguityFlags: string[];
  parserConfidence?: number;
  userConfirmed: false;
}
Important rules:

Keep the original user sentence.
Show the parsed fields in an editable review card.
Do not infer a water volume from duration alone without flow rate, emitter output, irrigated area or another valid conversion input.
Do not treat a recurring routine as proof of a completed irrigation event.
Unconfirmed ambiguous values cannot silently enter a calculation.
Record whether an entry is a routine, a planned action or an event that actually happened.
3.4 Analysis pipeline
Implement an actual job pipeline. Do not animate a list of stages on a timer.

REQUESTED
  → VALIDATING_SCOPE
  → SNAPSHOTTING_INPUTS
  → FETCHING_DATA
  → VALIDATING_AND_NORMALISING
  → SPATIAL_AGGREGATION
  → CALCULATING_INDICATORS
  → EVALUATING_FIELD_RULES
  → BUILDING_FIELD_ASSESSMENTS
  → BUILDING_FARM_SUMMARY
  → COMPARING_PREVIOUS_REPORT
  → GENERATING_EXPLANATIONS
  → VALIDATING_REPORT
  → PERSISTING_REPORT
  → COMPLETE | PARTIAL | FAILED | CANCELLED
Each stage must have a real status:

type StageStatus =
  | "queued"
  | "running"
  | "complete"
  | "partial"
  | "skipped"
  | "failed"
  | "cancelled";
The UI must display actual provider/job events. A provider is not marked complete until its response is validated and persisted. Optional source failure may lead to a partial report only if the remaining evidence is sufficient. A required input failure must block the affected calculation and explain why.

Pipeline steps
Validate the farm, field, geometry and profile IDs.
Capture immutable versions of farm, field, geometry, soil/crop profiles, farming practices and selected scope.
Resolve explicit user consent and which external requests are allowed.
Refresh supported providers in parallel with bounded timeouts and retry rules.
Parse and validate dates, units, ranges, fill values, quality flags and response schemas.
Normalise units while preserving original values and metadata.
Determine whether each source's pixel/grid resolution is appropriate for the selected field.
Perform polygon-weighted aggregation only where the geometry and raster resolution support it.
Calculate only indicators whose required inputs exist.
Run documented, versioned field risk rules.
Create each field's assessment.
Create the farm-wide summary without hiding severe field-level issues.
Compare with the previous compatible report.
Generate evidence-linked recommendations.
Ask AI to explain the validated findings, if AI is enabled.
Validate the final report schema, ranges, IDs, references and contradictions.
Persist an immutable report and update the farm's latest-report pointer only after successful persistence.
3.5 Deep Analysis report content
The report must contain the following sections. Each section starts with a short plain-language explanation and expands into technical details when requested.

A. Overall farm picture
Farm condition label, if valid.
Health score only if a defined and validated health model exists.
Data coverage and evidence confidence shown separately.
Number of fields assessed, needing attention, unavailable or not assessed.
Main strengths.
Main concerns.
Highest-priority next action.
Analysis date, selected scope and report version.
B. Field-by-field assessment
For each field show:

Field name and area.
Current crop and stage only if confirmed/supported.
Condition label.
Evidence coverage/confidence.
Leading finding.
Recommended next action.
Last observation/update date.
Link to the corresponding field in the existing farm workspace (for example, its field overview or map location). Do not create or link to a separate Field Analysis page.
C. Water conditions
Include only supported values:

Reference evapotranspiration (ET₀), with source/method.
Crop coefficient (Kc), crop stage and coefficient source where known.
Estimated crop evapotranspiration/demand (ETc), mm/day and selected-period total.
Precipitation, with source and period.
Effective rainfall, separately labelled as calculated/modelled.
Confirmed farmer-recorded irrigation events and amounts.
Saved irrigation routine as an assumption, not actual event history.
Surface and root-zone moisture, with separate depth/product labels.
Plant-available water (PAW), total available water (TAW), readily available water (RAW) and depletion only when soil hydraulic inputs and crop/rooting assumptions support these calculations.
Net water balance with explicit sign convention.
Potential unmet demand/stress interval only if the water-balance model is valid.
A daily calculation table with date, ET₀, Kc, ETc, precipitation, effective rainfall, irrigation, drainage/runoff terms if modelled, estimated storage/depletion and source status.
Never call a temperature-only estimate FAO-56 Penman–Monteith. Never infer actual irrigation from a routine. Never say rainfall automatically means irrigation did not happen.

D. Soil and land
Potential properties, only where a real dataset or farmer/lab measurement supplies them:

Sand, silt and clay percentages and textural class.
pH and measurement depth.
Soil organic carbon or organic matter, with conversion method stated if converted.
Bulk density.
Coarse fragments.
Cation exchange capacity (CEC).
Total nitrogen where available.
Available water capacity or water-retention values where available.
Electrical conductivity/salinity only from an appropriate source/measurement.
Drainage class only if sourced or clearly identified as an inference.
Elevation/slope from a suitable terrain dataset.
Lab test date, method, sample depth and lab/source.
Mapped/modelled estimate uncertainty.
If a mapped estimate differs from a newer field/lab test, show both. Do not overwrite the measured value. Mark the depth and evidence type prominently.

E. Vegetation and crop information
Only display satellite vegetation indicators if a real optical product has been retrieved and processed:

NDVI/EVI or another explicitly calculated index.
Product/sensor and band information.
Scene/composite date.
Spatial pixel size.
Cloud/quality mask result.
Valid pixel count and percentage of field area represented.
Field polygon aggregation method (mean, median, weighted statistic).
Historical baseline method and observation dates, where relevant to the finding.
Comparison with crop stage only where crop/date information is reliable.
Use careful wording such as Vegetation index is below this field's recent baseline. Do not claim disease detected, pest detected or crop is dying from NDVI alone.

F. Weather and climate exposure
Current conditions and forecast shown separately.
Historical daily/minimum/maximum/mean temperature.
Daily, weekly, monthly and seasonal precipitation totals.
Solar radiation, wind and relative humidity where supplied.
Reference ET₀ where the calculation inputs exist.
Growing Degree Days (GDD) only with crop-specific base/upper temperature rules and a declared method.
Heat/cold stress days only when crop, stage, thresholds and dates support the result.
Dry-spell length and rainfall anomaly against a defined local/seasonal baseline.
Historical dry/typical/wet seasons only if there are enough comparable historical years.
A forecast is not an observation. Reanalysis is not an exact weather-station observation. NASA POWER history is not a forward forecast.

G. Risks and potential issues
Every finding needs:

Risk title and severity.
Affected farm/field/crop/stage.
Trigger rule and version.
Evidence IDs, values, units and time window.
Source and quality status.
Historical baseline or threshold.
Possible explanation/mechanism.
Alternative explanations.
Confidence and missing evidence.
What could confirm or disprove the finding.
Mitigation options or practical next checks.
Only create a formal risk when a documented rule/threshold is satisfied. If evidence merely suggests something worth looking at, label it Observation or Watch point, not a confirmed risk.

H. Recommended actions
Every action must be linked to a finding or a documented agronomic rule and include:

Action title and clear verb.
Why it is suggested.
Affected field and crop.
Priority.
Suggested due window.
Required tools/information if known.
Evidence references.
Status: Suggested, Accepted, Scheduled, In progress, Completed, Deferred or Dismissed.
Completing a task does not prove the underlying risk is resolved. The next assessment must check the condition again.

I. Compare with previous report
Classify changes as:

New finding.
Continuing finding.
Resolved/clear under the current rule.
Improved since the previous comparable report.
Worsened since the previous comparable report.
Not comparable.
Change caused by new input, source availability, source version, geometry/profile edit or calculation-model version.
Never attribute an improvement to a farmer action unless the evidence supports that interpretation. If inputs, methods or source products differ materially, show a comparability warning instead of presenting raw score deltas as a real-world change.

J. Evidence and calculation explorer
Every important report value should open a shared evidence drawer displaying:

Metric name, value and unit.
Farm/field and geometry version.
Observation period and retrieval time.
Provider/product and source link.
Source type: measured, farmer-reported, satellite-derived, modelled, reanalysis, forecast, estimate or calculated.
Spatial resolution, pixel count/coverage and aggregation method.
Quality flags and missing-value handling.
Formula and input values.
Model/crop database/calculation version.
Confidence and known limitations.
4. DATA COLLECTION — SOURCES, APIS AND FALLBACK STRATEGY
The system must use provider adapters. Do not scatter raw HTTP requests through React components. All API secrets and authenticated data access stay server-side.

4.1 Provider adapter interface
Use a typed interface similar to:

interface ProviderRequest {
  farmId: string;
  fieldId?: string;
  geometry?: GeoJSON.Polygon | GeoJSON.MultiPolygon;
  centroid: { lat: number; lon: number };
  from?: string;
  to?: string;
  variables: string[];
  purpose: "analysis" | "refresh";
}

interface ObservationEnvelope<TValue = number> {
  observationId: string;
  providerId: string;
  productId: string;
  variable: string;
  value: TValue;
  unit: string;
  evidenceType:
    | "station_measurement"
    | "farmer_reported"
    | "satellite_derived"
    | "modelled"
    | "reanalysis"
    | "forecast"
    | "estimated"
    | "calculated";
  observedAt?: string;
  validFrom?: string;
  validTo?: string;
  retrievedAt: string;
  spatialResolution?: number;
  temporalResolution?: string;
  geometryId?: string;
  pixelCount?: number;
  validCoveragePct?: number;
  qualityFlags: string[];
  uncertainty?: { lower?: number; upper?: number; method?: string };
  sourceUrl: string;
  sourceVersion?: string;
  rawRecordRef: string;
  status: "valid" | "suspect" | "missing" | "stale";
}

interface DataProvider {
  id: string;
  getStatus(): Promise<ProviderHealth>;
  fetch(request: ProviderRequest): Promise<ObservationEnvelope[]>;
}
Each adapter must validate its response against a runtime schema (for example Zod), convert units at the ingestion boundary, preserve the raw provider response or a durable source reference, and return structured errors rather than swallowing failures.

4.2 Provider registry and recommended integrations
Treat this as the recommended acquisition plan. Verify current endpoints, variables, access restrictions, quotas, licensing and product versions during implementation. A provider name in this document is not evidence that it is already connected.

A. Weather forecast — Open-Meteo Forecast API
Primary candidate: https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast

Use it for short-range forecast data, not historical records. Request only the required variables, for example:

temperature_2m
relative_humidity_2m
precipitation
precipitation_probability
wind_speed_10m
wind_direction_10m
shortwave_radiation
et0_fao_evapotranspiration where available and verified in the current documentation
relevant daily min/max/sum variables
The current API documentation describes forecasts up to 16 days, depending on the selected model/product. Store model/provider, issue/retrieval time, timezone and valid time. Never store a forecast value as observed rainfall.

Commercial-use rule: Open-Meteo's free endpoint is restricted to non-commercial use and has published request limits. If Field Shift becomes a commercial product or needs guaranteed service/volume, obtain the appropriate paid licence/API plan or replace the provider with a licensed alternative. Include attribution where required.

Fallback: use an official national meteorological service for the farm's country when a supported API is available. A configurable global model adapter such as NOAA/ECMWF data may be considered after access method, licensing and processing are verified. Do not silently splice two different forecast models into one continuous series. Show a provider/model change marker.

Official references:

https://open-meteo.com/en/docs
https://open-meteo.com/en/terms
https://open-meteo.com/en/pricing
B. Historical weather and climate — NASA POWER Daily API
Primary candidate: NASA POWER Daily point API.

Base path:

https://power.larc.nasa.gov/api/temporal/daily/point
Use the official parameter catalogue to select and verify exact parameter IDs, community, supported date range, units and temporal definitions. Candidate variable groups include:

Daily minimum/maximum/mean air temperature.
Precipitation.
Solar radiation.
Wind.
Relative humidity.
Other supported agroclimatology variables required by the calculation.
Do not assume a parameter is available simply because its name sounds familiar. Confirm the ID and units against the current POWER API parameter catalogue before implementation.

NASA POWER Daily provides daily solar/meteorological time series, but its spatial resolution is source-dependent; the documentation warns about a 0.5° × 0.5° global grid for its catalog context. This is climate-grid information, not a field weather station. Preserve its native spatial resolution and time standard. Do not claim exact field-scale conditions from a grid that is much larger than the farm.

Use the Daily endpoint for day-level historical data where available. Use the Monthly or Climatology endpoints only for monthly/climatological results and label them accordingly. Do not expand monthly values into fabricated daily measurements.

Historical alternative/cross-check: Open-Meteo Historical Weather API using ERA5/ERA5-Land reanalysis. Its documentation describes ERA5 at approximately 0.25° and ERA5-Land at approximately 0.1° (~9 km), with historical coverage extending back decades. Reanalysis is model-assisted historical context, not a station measurement. Preserve which dataset was used; do not blend NASA POWER and ERA5 values without a documented source-selection rule.

Official references:

https://power.larc.nasa.gov/docs/services/api/temporal/daily/
https://power.larc.nasa.gov/docs/services/api/temporal/
https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/historical-weather-api
C. Soil properties — SoilGrids, with an essential access fallback
Preferred dataset: ISRIC SoilGrids global soil property maps. Use its mapped estimates for properties actually provided by the product, such as texture fractions, pH, soil organic carbon, bulk density and other documented soil layers/properties. Respect its depth intervals, map resolution and prediction uncertainty. It is a mapped prediction, not a lab test from the user's field.

Critical current status: ISRIC currently reports that the SoilGrids REST API has been temporarily paused. Do not build the production implementation so it depends on rest.isric.org being available.

Preferred access alternative: use the documented SoilGrids Web Coverage Service (WCS) for spatial subsets or WebDAV/global raster downloads, then store/cache the licensed source rasters on the backend and perform polygon sampling internally. Check current WCS/WebDAV availability and terms before deployment.

Secondary global fallback: FAO/IIASA Harmonized World Soil Database (HWSD) v2.0. It offers global soil information at approximately 1 km and multiple soil layers. This is coarser and not interchangeable with SoilGrids. Label the product, version and resolution, and reduce confidence accordingly.

Best field-specific source: user-provided soil-laboratory tests or a verified national/regional soil survey. Store sample date, sampling depth, method, lab, units and location/field association. A lab result should not be overwritten by a global map estimate. If a mapped estimate and lab value differ, show both and explain which one is used for which calculation.

If all sources fail: retain existing valid soil records with their dates and stale label. Do not invent pH, salinity, texture, nutrient deficiency or water-holding capacity.

Official references:

https://isric.org/explore/soilgrids
https://docs.isric.org/globaldata/soilgrids/
https://docs.isric.org/globaldata/soilgrids/SoilGrids_faqs_02.html
https://www.fao.org/land-water/resources/tools/databases/hwsd/en
D. Surface/root-zone soil moisture — NASA SMAP L4
Primary candidate: NASA SMAP Level-4 Global 3-hourly 9 km EASE-Grid Surface and Root Zone Soil Moisture, current version to be checked at implementation time.

The documented product assimilates SMAP radiometer observations into a land-surface model. It provides surface and root-zone soil-moisture estimates at roughly 9 km and a 3-hour time step. It is model-assisted gridded data, not a direct sensor reading from the exact field.

Use NASA Earthdata/NSIDC DAAC data services and authentication for programmatic retrieval. Store product version, science version, surface-vs-root-zone variable, grid cell, observation time, latency, quality flags and retrieval date. Confirm the correct access/subsetting service before coding.

Fallback: Open-Meteo historical ERA5-Land soil-water/soil-moisture variables or a correctly identified NASA POWER modelled root-zone moisture variable such as GWETROOT, if the chosen parameter and units are available and verified. These are alternatives with different model semantics. Never call them SMAP observed. Label the actual fallback product and lower confidence/coverage.

Field-scale rule: at 9 km, one cell may cover many small farms. If different fields fall in the same grid cell, the value cannot distinguish those fields. Display it as regional/root-zone context, not a precise field-level contrast.

Official references:

https://nsidc.org/data/spl4smgp/versions/8
https://nsidc.org/data/user-resources/data-announcements/data-set-updates-smap-level-4
https://nsidc.org/data/user-resources/help-center/what-data-subsetting-reformatting-and-reprojection-services-are-available-smap-data
E. Satellite vegetation — Sentinel-2 Level-2A first
Primary candidate: Copernicus Sentinel-2 Level-2A surface reflectance.

Use the Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem (CDSE) STAC catalogue to search by:

Farm/field polygon (intersects).
Acquisition date/time range (datetime).
Collection sentinel-2-l2a where present in the current STAC catalogue.
Cloud cover metadata where available.
Current STAC endpoint:

https://stac.dataspace.copernicus.eu/v1/
Use CDSE openEO, Sentinel Hub processing APIs or a tested internal raster-processing pipeline to retrieve only the required assets and compute polygon statistics. Authenticate as required by CDSE. Avoid downloading whole scenes when an appropriate processing API can return a field subset/statistic.

Sentinel-2 Level-2A supplies bottom-of-atmosphere surface reflectance and a Scene Classification Layer. Red band B04 and NIR band B08 are 10 m; the Scene Classification Layer is 20 m. Use quality layers to mask clouds, cloud shadows, snow and invalid pixels. Do not use a simple cloud-cover percentage for the whole scene as a substitute for pixel-level masking over the selected field.

Core calculation:

NDVI = (NIR - RED) / (NIR + RED);
// Sentinel-2: NIR = B08, RED = B04
// Guard against NoData and denominator ≈ 0.
Optional EVI must be calculated only from the appropriate verified bands and exact published formula. Preserve source reflectance scale factors and processing metadata.

For each field/date, return NDVI/EVI distribution, median/mean as appropriate, valid pixel count, valid polygon coverage percentage, cloud/quality exclusions, date and product. The field's area and available valid pixels determine whether the statistic is usable.

Alternative 1: NASA Harmonized Landsat Sentinel-2 (HLS v2.0) 30 m Cloud Optimized GeoTIFFs. NASA states that the data are distributed in Earthdata Cloud, require an Earthdata login and commonly have around 2–3 days latency depending on inputs. HLS can provide a consistent Landsat/Sentinel time series but has coarser pixels than Sentinel-2 native 10 m products.

Alternative 2: MODIS MOD13Q1 NDVI/EVI at 250 m with a 16-day composite. Use for larger areas and longer-term regional vegetation context only when field size and valid pixel count support it. Do not use 250 m MODIS to distinguish tiny adjacent fields.

No usable imagery: show No suitable cloud-free observation for this period or Field is too small for this product's resolution. Do not create NDVI from rainfall, weather, crop type or a placeholder value.

Official references:

https://documentation.dataspace.copernicus.eu/APIs/STAC.html
https://documentation.dataspace.copernicus.eu/Data/SentinelMissions/Sentinel2.html
https://sentinels.copernicus.eu/documents/d/sentinel/sentinel-2-products-specification-document-15_1
https://hls.gsfc.nasa.gov/data-access-and-tools/
https://ladsweb.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/missions-and-measurements/products/MOD13Q1/
F. Precipitation cross-check — NASA GPM IMERG
Use NASA GPM IMERG where the analysis needs a satellite precipitation cross-check. Choose the correct Early, Late or Final product for the use case and show latency/quality differences. IMERG offers different accumulation products, including half-hourly, three-hourly and daily products. Common grid spacing is approximately 0.1°; it is not a rain gauge at the field.

Do not blindly average IMERG rainfall with POWER/ERA5/Open-Meteo precipitation. Choose a documented primary series for each calculation. Use other sources as independent checks; if they disagree materially, surface the disagreement and reduce confidence.

Access through the current NASA GPM data distribution/approved NASA Earthdata service. Confirm the product version, file/API access, authentication, latency and units. If unavailable, use the configured historical weather/precipitation source as a clearly labelled alternative, not as the same product.

Official reference:

https://gpm.nasa.gov/data/imerg
https://gpm.nasa.gov/data/directory
G. Terrain — NASADEM or a documented DEM alternative
Primary candidate: NASADEM_HGT v001, approximately 30 m global DEM, with coverage limitations (approximately 60°N to 56°S). Use Earthdata/LP DAAC data access and verify tiles/coverage.

Derive elevation summaries and slope only after applying proper projection and vertical/horizontal unit handling. Use terrain to contextualise elevation, slope and possible runoff/erosion susceptibility. Terrain alone does not prove waterlogging, erosion or drainage failure.

Fallback: Copernicus DEM GLO-30 or an appropriate documented SRTM/other DEM product for areas where NASADEM is unavailable. Preserve the chosen DEM name/version/vertical datum and do not blend elevation rasters with different datums without transformation.

Official reference:

https://doi.org/10.5067/MEaSUREs/NASADEM/NASADEM_HGT.001
https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/analyse/apis
H. Evapotranspiration support — FAO-56 and MODIS ET
Preferred agronomic calculation: implement FAO-56 reference evapotranspiration ET₀ using the Penman–Monteith method only when the required meteorological inputs and units are present and compatible.

Reference equation:

ET0 = [0.408 × Δ × (Rn − G) + γ × (900 / (T + 273)) × u2 × (es − ea)]
      / [Δ + γ × (1 + 0.34 × u2)]
Where the implementation must define and correctly derive:

Δ: slope of saturation vapour pressure curve.
Rn: net radiation.
G: soil heat flux.
γ: psychrometric constant.
T: mean air temperature at 2 m.
u2: wind speed at 2 m, converted from measurement height where needed.
es - ea: vapour-pressure deficit term.
Use the exact FAO-56 conventions, day/time units, pressure/elevation corrections and input requirements. Validate against reference examples. If required inputs are missing, use a separately named approximate method or return unavailable. Never claim a temperature-only method is FAO-56.

Crop evapotranspiration:

ETc = Kc × ET0
Use a sourced crop coefficient (Kc) for the relevant crop and growth stage. Use locally suitable crop data where available; FAO-56 crop tables are a starting reference, not a universal replacement for locally validated coefficients. Store crop, variety if known, stage, initial/mid/late stage lengths, source and any climate adjustment. If the coefficient or stage cannot be supported, mark ETc unavailable/approximate with the reason.

Independent satellite context: NASA MOD16A2GF is an 8-day, 500 m gap-filled MODIS ET product. It can be used as broader actual-ET context or a cross-check for sufficiently large fields; it is far too coarse to pretend it is a precise measurement for every small field. Its product algorithm uses a Penman–Monteith approach with meteorological and MODIS inputs. Preserve product and quality metadata.

Official references:

https://www.fao.org/4/X0490E/X0490E00.htm
https://ladsweb.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/missions-and-measurements/products/MOD16A2GF
4.3 Source priority and fallback ladder
Apply a variable-specific source order. Do not define one global fallback provider for every metric.

Example:

const sourcePriority = {
  measuredSoilTest: ["user_confirmed_lab_result", "national_soil_survey", "soilgrids_raster", "fao_hwsd"],
  weatherForecast: ["open_meteo_forecast_or_licensed_provider", "official_national_meteorological_api", "other_verified_nwp_provider"],
  historicalClimate: ["nasa_power_daily", "era5_land_reanalysis", "other_verified_reanalysis"],
  soilMoisture: ["smap_l4", "era5_land_soil_water", "nasa_power_modelled_moisture"],
  vegetation: ["sentinel2_l2a", "nasa_hls", "modis_mod13q1_if_resolution_supports_field"],
  rainfallCrossCheck: ["gpm_imerg", "verified_precipitation_reanalysis_or_power"]
} as const;
This is an order of preference, not a rule to mix all values. The selected source must be suitable for the metric, geography, time interval, licensing and required resolution.

When a provider fails:

Record the provider error and request time.
Retry only retryable errors with bounded exponential backoff.
Check whether an allowed, semantically appropriate alternative exists.
If it does, retrieve and label the alternative with its actual source/product semantics.
Lower coverage/confidence where the alternative is coarser, less current or otherwise weaker.
If no suitable alternative exists, preserve the last valid observation as stale or show unavailable.
Never fill missing data with a synthetic number, zero, previous value without a stale label, rainfall-derived NDVI or a fictional status.
5. SPATIAL PROCESSING — HOW DATA IS APPLIED TO A FARM OR FIELD
A large share of the data sources return rasters/grids. Field Shift must process them against the saved farm/field geometry correctly.

5.1 Geometry prerequisites
Use the saved validated farm/field polygon, not the draggable map centre pin.
Use GeoJSON with a declared CRS at API boundaries (WGS84 / EPSG:4326).
Validate polygon closure, at least three unique vertices, self-intersection, duplicate vertices, plausible extent and non-zero area.
Reproject to a suitable projected/equal-area CRS for area/intersection calculations.
Preserve a geometryVersionId and use it in every raster-sampling result/report.
A changed geometry invalidates the spatial summaries derived from the old polygon, but does not alter old reports.
5.2 Raster polygon statistics
For each data product:

Load its native grid and metadata.
Intersect it with the selected field polygon.
Apply provider quality flags and NoData masks before calculating statistics.
Calculate pixel-field overlap weights where applicable.
Compute valid pixel count and valid field-area coverage.
Calculate a documented statistic (mean, median, percentile or distribution) appropriate to the metric.
Preserve pixel size, grid cell IDs, acquisition dates and quality flags.
Store the sampling method and geometry version.
Return data-quality fields such as:

interface SpatialCoverage {
  fieldAreaM2: number;
  sourcePixelSizeM?: number;
  intersectingPixelCount: number;
  validPixelCount: number;
  validAreaM2: number;
  validCoveragePct: number;
  aggregation: "area_weighted_mean" | "median" | "percentile" | "nearest_cell" | "not_applicable";
  spatialFit: "field_scale" | "coarse_context" | "insufficient";
  geometryVersionId: string;
}
5.3 Resolution gate
Before displaying a result as field-specific, compare the data's native pixel/grid size with the field area and shape.

If a product pixel is much larger than the field, mark it Regional context or Coarse grid estimate.
If several fields share one coarse cell, do not imply that they have independently measured different soil-moisture values.
If the raster contains too few valid pixels or too little valid field coverage, suppress the field-level metric or lower its status to Insufficient coverage according to a tested product-specific threshold.
Thresholds for valid pixels/coverage must be stored in product configuration and validated; do not pick a threshold ad hoc inside a component.
A visually detailed map does not make a coarse dataset more accurate.
