6.1 Weather calculations
Where daily input values exist and are valid, calculate:

Minimum, maximum and mean temperature.
Daily/weekly/monthly/seasonal precipitation totals.
Solar radiation totals/averages.
Wind/humidity statistics where supplied.
ET₀ where the required inputs/method are available.
Hot/cold day counts only with documented crop-specific thresholds and relevant crop-stage dates.
Dry spell duration from a declared precipitation threshold and time step.
Historical anomalies against a defined local/seasonal baseline.
Growing Degree Days using the crop's documented base/upper thresholds and chosen method.
Example GDD structure:

interface GddConfig {
  baseTempC: number;
  upperTempC?: number;
  method: "single_sine" | "capped_mean" | "other_documented";
  sourceId: string;
  version: string;
}
Do not apply a universal GDD threshold to all crops. Record whether the calculation used daily observed-like/reanalysis data or a coarser monthly approximation. If only monthly climate means are available, do not present the output as exact daily accumulated GDD.

6.2 Water balance and crop water demand
Implement water calculations only when relevant inputs are present. The engine must support the following concepts, but it must not fabricate missing parameters:

ET₀ — reference crop evapotranspiration.
Kc — crop coefficient by growth stage.
ETc — crop evapotranspiration/demand.
P — precipitation.
Peff — effective precipitation, which is calculated using a named method and is not equal to raw rainfall.
I — confirmed irrigation depth/amount converted to a field-area basis.
RO — runoff, only if modelled.
DP — deep percolation/drainage, only if modelled.
CR — capillary rise, only if modelled.
Rooting depth Zr and allowable depletion fraction p where supported.
Soil water at field capacity and wilting point (or a valid alternative soil hydraulic representation).
A daily root-zone depletion equation may follow the FAO-56 convention:

Dr(i) = Dr(i-1) - (P(i) - RO(i)) - I(i) - CR(i) + ETc(i) + DP(i)
Use a sign convention documented in code and the Evidence Drawer. The implementation must reconcile the exact FAO-56 definitions, initial storage and boundary conditions before calling the model FAO-56 compliant.

Plant-available water calculations, where inputs are supported:

TAW = 1000 × (θFC − θWP) × Zr
RAW = p × TAW
Where volumetric water content is dimensionless and Zr is in metres, yielding millimetres. Do not calculate TAW/RAW if field capacity, wilting point, root depth or units are unknown/unsupported.

Output:

Demand versus supply chart.
Water balance by day/time step.
Confirmed irrigation events separately from saved routine.
Observed/modelled precipitation separately from forecast.
Surface and root-zone moisture in separate series.
Assumptions, missing terms and confidence.
Stress intervals only if the crop/soil model supports them.
Do not convert hours watered into mm unless the flow and irrigated area are known. Do not claim the model knows what physically happened on the farm if the farmer did not record it and no suitable sensor exists.

6.3 Soil calculations
Parse source property, depth interval, unit, date and quality.
Convert units explicitly and store original values.
Derive texture class only from valid sand/silt/clay fractions that satisfy the selected classification's requirements.
Name the classification system (e.g. USDA texture triangle).
pH interpretation must be crop/context-specific and sourced.
Soil organic carbon must not silently become organic matter; if conversion is used, record the conversion factor and its basis.
Salinity/EC must come from an appropriate source or direct measurement; do not infer it from texture or pH alone.
Drainage and erosion findings must be sourced or explicitly marked as cautious inference.
A coarse soil grid must not be presented as a laboratory result.
6.4 Vegetation time series
Calculate each index from the correct reflectance bands and scale factors.
Apply cloud, cloud-shadow, snow and invalid-data masks before aggregation.
Use a consistent field polygon version.
Store raw observation date, composite interval, valid-pixel fraction and source.
Compare only compatible index products/time windows or explicitly normalise/mark a source change.
For seasonal baselines, align crop stage/season where possible.
No reliable historical baseline and Insufficient valid imagery are valid outputs.
6.5 Risk and status engine
Create versioned rule definitions such as:

interface RiskRule {
  id: string;
  version: string;
  name: string;
  metricDependencies: string[];
  cropContextRequired: boolean;
  stageContextRequired: boolean;
  thresholdConfig: Record<string, number | string>;
  minimumCoverage: number;
  minimumConfidence: number;
  severity: "info" | "low" | "moderate" | "high" | "critical";
  evidenceRequirements: string[];
  actionTemplateIds: string[];
}
Risk classes may include heat exposure, cold/frost exposure, water deficit/drought, heavy precipitation/flooding/waterlogging indicators, soil limitations and unusual vegetation-index readings relative to a valid baseline. Pest/disease risks must not be generated solely from NDVI or a generic AI interpretation; they require a validated model or a clearly labelled farmer report/watch point.

Thresholds must be crop-, location-, stage- and metric-specific where the evidence supports this. Store rule version and trigger evidence. If no defensible threshold exists, display an observation, not a formal alert.

6.6 Farm and field status aggregation
Preferred field status labels:

Good — enough current evidence is available and no configured material concern is triggered.
Monitor — a meaningful signal deserves observation, but evidence does not justify an urgent risk label.
Needs Attention — one or more validated concerns require investigation/action.
Alert — a high-priority validated rule has triggered.
Insufficient Data — required evidence is missing, too coarse, stale or incompatible.
Not Analysed — no report exists.
A numeric composite health score is optional and must not ship until its components, weights, scale, missing-data policy, calibration and validation are scientifically reviewed. If implemented, store its model version and component breakdown. Show Health, Evidence Coverage and Confidence separately.

At farm level, report counts and a carefully defined aggregate. Do not allow many healthy fields to cancel out one severe field. The top farm label must respect the highest material field-level alert under the documented aggregation rules.

7. REPORTS & ANALYSIS HISTORY
