7.4 Compare two reports
Provide a side-by-side comparison view:

Overall farm summary and field summaries.
New, resolved, continuing and changed findings.
Metric deltas where comparable.
Changed farm/field inputs.
Changed geometry/profile versions.
Changed data providers or source resolution.
Changed calculation/rule/model versions.
Coverage/confidence changes.
Actions accepted/completed/deferred since the earlier report.
Classify each apparent change as:

type ChangeCause =
  | "environmental_observation_change"
  | "farmer_input_change"
  | "management_event_change"
  | "geometry_change"
  | "source_or_coverage_change"
  | "calculation_version_change"
  | "unknown_or_not_attributable";
If two reports are not comparable, still allow opening each report but block misleading numeric delta charts and explain why.

7.5 Export
Where implemented:

PDF: human-readable report with summary, findings, actions, per-field sections, sources and limitations.
CSV: tabular observations/metrics with units, timestamps, source IDs and field IDs.
JSON: structured report snapshot with schema version and provenance.
Exports must use the saved report snapshot, not run a new analysis while downloading. Clearly show Preparing, Ready, Failed and retry states. Do not expose private coordinates in a general report unless the export explicitly includes geometry and the owner confirms the privacy implications.

8. AUTOMATION & MONITORING
8.1 Purpose
Allow a farm owner to schedule recurring Deep Analysis so they do not have to manually request every reassessment.

8.2 Important architecture requirement
A browser-only website cannot reliably run analysis while closed. Real automation requires:

A durable schedule record.
A server-side scheduler/cron trigger.
A durable job queue.
A worker that executes the same analysis pipeline as a manual run.
Durable report storage.
Notification delivery or an in-app notification record.
Consent and deletion controls.
Do not simulate this with setInterval, a browser timer or an open-tab requirement while claiming the analysis runs automatically.

8.3 Schedule creation flow
The Create schedule flow must collect:

Farm ID.
Scope: whole farm, all fields, selected fields or one field.
Selected field IDs where relevant.
Frequency: daily, every 3 days, weekly; custom interval only if implemented safely.
Preferred run time.
Farm timezone (not merely browser timezone).
Whether to refresh all supported data or a selected subset.
Whether to use saved farming routines and when to ask for reconfirmation.
Notification preferences.
Explicit consent for any farm data/geometry/input snapshot sent to a remote backend or external API.
Before activation, show exactly what will be sent remotely, why it is needed, where it is processed and how the user can pause/revoke it. Remote/background processing must be opt-in, not silently enabled.

8.4 Schedule list/dashboard
Display:

Schedule name/farm.
Scope and included fields.
Frequency/timezone.
Next run time.
Last run time/result.
Current state.
Consent state.
Actions: Pause, Resume, Run now, Edit, History, Delete.
Run now must reuse an already running/queued job for the same scope/input snapshot where appropriate, rather than creating duplicate costly runs.

8.5 Job lifecycle
type JobStatus =
  | "queued"
  | "running"
  | "complete"
  | "partial"
  | "failed"
  | "retrying"
  | "cancelled"
  | "skipped";

interface AnalysisJob {
  id: string;
  scheduleId?: string;
  farmId: string;
  scope: AnalysisScope;
  requestedAt: string;
  startedAt?: string;
  finishedAt?: string;
  status: JobStatus;
  currentStage?: string;
  attempt: number;
  idempotencyKey: string;
  reportId?: string;
  errorCode?: string;
  errorMessage?: string;
}
Use idempotency keys to prevent duplicate reports/notifications after retry. Use bounded retries with exponential backoff for transient provider/server errors. Do not endlessly retry invalid farm profiles or unsupported inputs.

8.6 Monitoring and notification rules
Notify the user only when the job reaches a meaningful final state or a new important finding is produced.

Potential notifications:

Scheduled analysis completed.
Scheduled analysis partial/failed.
New high-priority finding.
Existing finding materially worsened or improved.
A source has been unavailable for a sustained period and materially affects the assessment.
Farming routine needs reconfirmation.
Avoid notification spam:

Deduplicate repeated alerts for the same unresolved risk.
Do not send alerts for ordinary score fluctuations within expected variability.
A source failure normally creates a data-quality notice, not a crop-risk alert.
Respect notification preferences and quiet hours for non-critical messages.
Pause stops future runs but retains report history. Delete removes the schedule. Revoking background-processing consent must stop future remote jobs and delete or queue deletion of any remote analysis snapshot according to the disclosed retention policy. Do not say deletion is complete until the server confirms it.

9. AI AGRONOMIST — HOW AI MAY BE USED INSIDE ADVANCED
AI is a supporting explanation layer, not the scientific engine.

Permitted uses:

Explain a validated finding in simple or advanced language.
Explain why a metric changed, using report comparison metadata.
Summarise the evidence behind a risk.
Explain a calculation using the actual formula and substituted values.
Parse natural-language farming routines/observations into a draft for user confirmation.
Answer questions about the selected farm/field/report.
Guide the user to the relevant section or evidence drawer.
Forbidden behaviours:

Invent measurements, dates, source responses or scores.
Claim disease, pest or crop failure without a validated supporting model.
Override a deterministic crop/water/soil calculation.
Change farm records, schedules, irrigation history, crop plans or report contents without explicit user action.
Treat a farmer's routine as a completed event.
Treat community statements as verified facts.
Claim an external API is available without its real connector state.
9.1 AI request context
Send only the minimum required context, such as:

interface AgronomistContext {
  farmId: string;
  fieldId?: string;
  reportId?: string;
  rotationPlanId?: string;
  evidenceRefs: string[];
  findings: ValidatedFindingSummary[];
  userMode: "simple" | "advanced";
  locale: string;
}
Do not send the entire farm archive, unrelated journal notes or all farm coordinates by default. Quantitative AI claims must reference report/evidence IDs. Validate structured output and render text safely. If AI is unavailable, the deterministic report remains usable and says Explanation unavailable; the measured/calculated results are still available.

