12.3 Provider infrastructure
Implement a provider registry and shared network client with:

Per-provider timeout.
Bounded retry with exponential backoff and jitter.
Rate limiting and provider-specific request budgets.
Circuit breaker/degraded state.
Response schema validation.
Request correlation ID.
Cache keyed by product/variable/grid/time range/version, with private farm results separated from public/shared cache.
Raw response/reference preservation.
Unit normalization.
Provider attribution and licensing metadata.
Per-variable fallback rules.
Never expose API credentials in frontend bundles, client-side environment variables, source maps or logs. Never send private farm geometry to a provider unless necessary for the requested processing and allowed by the user's consent.

12.4 Suggested connected backend when scheduled analysis is enabled
For durable automation, use an architecture equivalent to:

React/Vite frontend
   → Express/TypeScript API
      → PostgreSQL + PostGIS (farm, field, geometry, reports, schedules)
      → Redis + durable job queue (e.g. BullMQ) OR managed cloud task queue
      → Analysis worker(s)
          → provider adapters
          → deterministic calculation engine
          → report persistence
          → notification service
      → object storage for raw/processed raster assets and report exports
Use the existing stack where feasible; do not migrate the entire product solely to adopt a library. If a different queue/database/storage service is chosen, document equivalent durability, authorization, retry, retention and observability guarantees.

12.5 Caching and freshness
Cache key must include:

providerId + productId + variable + gridCell/geometry + timeWindow
+ units/aggregationVersion + productVersion
Use shorter TTL for current forecasts and frequently updated conditions; longer TTL for static terrain/soil products and immutable historical observations. A newer retrieval may still contain an old observation; display both dates. A failed refresh must not overwrite the last successful record with zero/null or a false recent timestamp.

13. ACCEPTANCE TESTS — DEFINITION OF DONE
Do not mark Advanced complete merely because the interface renders.

13.1 Deep Analysis tests
Whole-farm run includes every selected field and retains per-field results.
Single-field run is clearly labelled as a field-only assessment.
A farm with one critical field and several healthy fields still surfaces the critical field prominently.
Identical inputs and engine versions produce reproducible deterministic results.
Unknown soil/water values remain unknown; no default ideal values appear.
Manual analysis progress reflects actual job/provider events.
Failed optional source produces an explainable partial report only if remaining evidence is sufficient.
Failed required input blocks the affected metric with a useful explanation.
Previous successful report remains available after a failed newer run.
AI failure does not erase deterministic findings.
13.2 Provider/API tests
Test:

Successful request.
Timeout.
HTTP 429/rate limit.
HTTP 5xx.
Invalid JSON/schema.
Missing fields.
Fill values/NoData.
Unit mismatch.
Unknown parameter.
Stale result.
Provider product/version change.
Token/authentication failure.
Spatial coverage outside product bounds.
Raster with no valid pixels in the farm polygon.
Polygon smaller than source pixels.
Fallback provider with different resolution/semantics.
Assert that the UI shows the real status and source provenance. Never report a source as connected because it appears in a static list.

13.3 Scientific tests
FAO-56 ET₀ known examples pass within documented tolerances.
ETc uses sourced Kc and correct crop stage.
Rainfall, effective rainfall, forecast rainfall and irrigation remain separate.
Water balance uses correct units, sign convention and initial storage.
TAW/RAW unavailable if field capacity/wilting point/root depth inputs are missing.
GDD tests use crop-specific thresholds and documented cap method.
NDVI uses correct reflectance bands, scale factors and masks.
No NDVI is calculated from rainfall or precipitation.
Pixel/field coverage is computed after quality masking.
Soil texture classification only runs on valid compatible fractions.
Lab results remain distinct from gridded soil predictions.
Risk triggers use versioned thresholds and record their evidence IDs.
Confidence is separate from health/suitability scores.
13.4 Report-history tests
Old reports remain immutable after farm inputs change.
Report comparison distinguishes environmental change from input, source, geometry or model-version change.
Incompatible values are not compared as though they were equivalent.
Export uses the saved report snapshot and includes units/provenance.
13.5 Automation tests
A scheduled run executes with the browser closed when durable backend scheduling is configured.
Browser-only mode never claims that it can run in the background while closed.
Duplicate job submissions do not create duplicate reports.
Pause prevents future runs.
Resume restores future runs.
Delete and consent revocation have accurate pending/complete states.
Retry does not duplicate a successful report.
Timezone/daylight-saving behaviour is tested.
Old irrigation routine becomes Needs confirmation when policy requires it.
Observed rain never silently deletes or edits an irrigation event.
13.6 Cross-page tests
Farm Overview → Advanced → report → field → Map opens the exact farm/field.
Changing the selected farm changes every chart/table/assessment context coherently.
Crop names in findings/actions link to the correct field/rotation/crop occurrence where such references exist.
Accepted recommendations create/update Calendar actions without falsely resolving their source risk.
Farm Settings rename/delete/field edits follow existing farm data cascade policies.
13.7 Visual/accessibility tests
Validate at 1440px, 1280px, 1024px, 768px, 430px, 390px and 360px widths. Test keyboard-only operation, screen-reader names, visible focus, contrast, reduced motion, browser zoom, long farm names, many fields, long time ranges, empty data, source errors and large report tables. No chart labels may overlap; no page-wide horizontal overflow; every chart needs a textual/table alternative.

